{
  "dom_id": "step-q212073",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Let $A={1,2,3}$ and consider the relation \"is smaller than\" $(<)$ on $A.$ If this relation is given by the set $R,$ express $R$ as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(1,2),(1,$\n{{field-1}}$),($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)}$",
  "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x<y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=3$ satisfy $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(1,2),(1,3).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,3).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ then there is no value of  $y\\in A$ that satisfies $x<y.$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(1,2),(1,3),(2,3)}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
